for sarah navid the lifting of mandates  has come with some concerns while i  completely understand that society needs  to go back to whatever normal is  um  that also means that a huge chunk of our  vulnerable population is going to be  stuck at home for a longer period of  time navida's immuno compromised and  lives with a family member who's very  vulnerable so to protect herself and her  loved ones i try to avoid big crowds i  am still distancing and wearing masks  only seeing close friends and family and  if i need to go to a grocery store or  need to make a trip to the mall i try to  go during times when i know it's not  going to be crowded there are many  canadians like naveed who are very  vulnerable and trying to protect  themselves during the sixth wave a  statistics canada report finds 38 of  canadians and private households have  one or more underlying health conditions  making them more vulnerable to  complications after contracting covet  19. it's an extremely dangerous time  right now for people who are  immunosuppressed but medicine professor  don baudisch says there are ways you can  try and protect yourselves still being  up to date on your vaccines is  incredibly important if you're  immunocompromised having a high quality  mask and reducing social contacts not  going to big events and since many  children are not eligible for vaccines  some of the  highest infection rates are in children  so limiting contact with children would  be helpful and if you do contract covet  19 bowdish suggests reaching out to your  family doctor to see if you qualify for  an antiviral pill she says every little  bit helps in saving lives and minimizing  hospitalizations  charming samani global news
 

